close_reason: "First-party Epic Lore research and the native PM-linked attribution design are published in LORE.md, recorded in this PM item, and linked to scoped implementation features."
body: "# Epic Lore research and the pm-vcs design response\n\nStatus: architecture input, researched 2026-08-01 against EpicGames/lore `main`\nand release `v0.8.6`. Lore is still a pre-1.0 project; this document records the\nobserved design, not a compatibility promise.\n\n## Scope and name disambiguation\n\nThis document concerns **Epic Games' Lore VCS**, the Rust implementation at\n[EpicGames/lore](https://github.com/EpicGames/lore). It does not concern\n[lore.kernel.org](https://lore.kernel.org/), the Linux kernel community's email\narchive and patch-series workflow. Both influence pm-vcs, but in different ways:\n\n- Epic Lore informs storage, scale, file identity, sparse working copies,\n  remotes, locking, isolation and server architecture.\n- kernel lore and `git send-email` inform portable patch series and review\n  lifecycles that do not require a forge.\n\nThe names must never be used interchangeably in pm-vcs documentation or PM\nitems.\n\n## Primary sources\n\nThe research uses only Epic's canonical repository and first-party docs:\n\n- [Repository and implementation](https://github.com/EpicGames/lore)\n- [System design](https://epicgames.github.io/lore/explanation/system-design/)\n- [CLI command reference](https://epicgames.github.io/lore/reference/lore-cli-commands/)\n- [CLI configuration reference](https://epicgames.github.io/lore/reference/lore-cli-config/)\n- [Quickstart](https://epicgames.github.io/lore/tutorials/quickstart/)\n- [Glossary](https://epicgames.github.io/lore/glossary/)\n- [Roadmap](https://epicgames.github.io/lore/roadmap/)\n- [FastCDC decision](https://epicgames.github.io/lore/developing/decisions/00001-fast-cdc/)\n- [Mutable branch-state decision](https://epicgames.github.io/lore/developing/decisions/00002-branch-tracking-in-mutable-store/)\n- [View-independent merge decision](https://epicgames.github.io/lore/developing/decisions/00018-view-independent-merges-and-subtree-grafting/)\n\n## Executive conclusion\n\nLore validates pm-vcs's core premise that a modern VCS must be a complete system\nfor arbitrary files rather than a source-text database. It also exposes where\npm-vcs remains deliberately small today. pm-vcs already has an independent\ncontent-addressed object store, arbitrary byte blobs, trees, commits, refs,\nstaging, status, text diff/merge, structured-record merge, history rewriting,\nbundles, verification and an operation log. It now has stable file IDs and native PM-linked attribution. It does not yet have\nchunked large files, sparse/lazy materialization, remote storage, file locks,\nmulti-tenant authorization, repository composition, lifecycle deletion or a\nserved forge.\n\npm-vcs should adopt Lore's separation of immutable content from mutable pointers,\nbinary-first hot paths, fragment-aware scaling, explicit file identity, sparse\ninstances, resumable transfer and atomic remote publication. It should adapt\nLore's centralized server model into a federatable remote model: one remote is\nauthoritative for a branch update, but repositories and bundles remain usable\noffline and transferable without a permanent central service. It should retain\npm-vcs's native structured records, stable change IDs and operation log, which\nare differentiators rather than Lore features.\n\n## Lore's system model\n\n### Two systems behind one API\n\nLore separates a storage subsystem from a version-control subsystem. The storage\nlayer exposes immutable content-addressed fragments plus a small mutable key-value\nstore. It knows nothing about files, branches or revisions. The VCS layer builds\nMerkle trees, revisions, staging, branches, merges and sync on those primitives.\nThe public API is primary; the CLI and server are consumers of it.\n\nThis is a strong boundary for pm-vcs. `engine/objects.ts` and future pack/fragment\nbackends must remain usable without command handlers, while repository operations\nmust use the same exported TypeScript API as the pm extension. A command-only\ncapability is an architectural defect.\n\n### Immutable bytes and mutable names\n\nLore uses BLAKE3-addressed immutable fragments. Mutable branch and repository\nlookups live separately and advance with compare-and-swap. Branch identity is a\nstable opaque ID independent of its display name. A rename changes the mapping,\nnot the branch's identity.\n\npm-vcs already separates immutable objects from mutable refs and already requires\ncompare-and-swap for ref movement. It should add stable ref IDs before remote\nserving, so renames, archival and restoration do not make audit history depend on\na human-readable name.\n\n### Revisions and trees\n\nLore revisions point at Merkle-tree state and parent revisions. Fixed-size node\nand node-block layouts allow structural sharing and targeted traversal. Parent\nhashes make history tamper-evident. Branch, merge, rebase, cherry-pick and squash\nall produce immutable revisions.\n\npm-vcs already has canonical trees and commit DAGs. It should not copy Lore's byte\nformat: pm-vcs is independently specified and TypeScript-native. It should copy\nthe invariant that the on-disk and wire formats are versioned contracts, not\nincidental implementation details.\n\n### Arbitrary files and binary-first storage\n\nLore's storage and transport paths treat all content as opaque bytes. Text diff\nand merge are higher-level defaults. Binary-specific viewers and merge tools are\npluggable, while files that cannot be merged can use explicit locks.\n\npm-vcs already stores every unconfigured path as an arbitrary byte `blob` and\nnever requires UTF-8 at the storage boundary. The current loose-object store,\nhowever, treats a multi-gigabyte file as one object. General-VCS completeness\nrequires:\n\n1. streaming reads and writes that never materialize a whole large file;\n2. content-defined chunks for files where insertions should preserve deduplication;\n3. fixed-size chunks where random range access is more important;\n4. chunk-list objects with byte offsets for range reads;\n5. compression metadata separate from uncompressed content identity;\n6. binary diff/view/merge adapters above storage; and\n7. enforceable file locks for declared unmergeable paths.\n\nLore uses FastCDC for content-defined chunking and Zstandard compression. pm-vcs\nshould benchmark those choices in TypeScript before committing to algorithms or\nthresholds. The invariants matter more than copying constants.\n\n### Stable file identity\n\nLore carries a context beside a content hash, commonly a stable file ID. That\nidentity survives moves and copies and scopes lifecycle operations such as\nobliteration. A path says where a file is in one revision; the file ID says which\nlogical file it is across revisions.\n\npm-vcs now stores a stable `FileId` in every versioned file entry and resolves\nPM-linked files through native history. Moves preserve identity; copies mint a new\nidentity with source provenance; identity disagreements become explicit, resolvable\nmerge conflicts while the merged tree remains structurally valid. Historical\nPM-link events at very large scale remain a separate indexing boundary.\n\n### Dirty state and staging intent\n\nLore does not scan the filesystem for every status call. Editors or tools can mark\npaths dirty; `status --scan` reconciles the filesystem when required. Staging is\nrecorded intent, and fragmentation/hashing can be deferred until commit. This\nkeeps routine work proportional to the changed set.\n\npm-vcs's stat cache already avoids rereading stable files, while an explicit add\nhashes content into the index. The Lore model suggests a later two-tier index:\n\n- dirty intent: cheap, mutable, path/file-ID scoped;\n- staged snapshot: immutable content IDs used by the next commit.\n\nThe staged snapshot remains necessary for partial staging and reproducible\ncommits. Dirty hints may optimize discovery but must never be authoritative;\n`--scan` and metadata verification remain the recovery boundary.\n\n### Sparse and lazy instances\n\nLore instances materialize a view rather than assuming every clone contains the\nwhole repository. View rules control inbound materialization; ignore rules control\noutbound staging. Missing fragments are fetched lazily and cached in a shared\nstore that multiple working trees can reuse.\n\npm-vcs should add repository views only after remote reads and fragment storage\nexist. A view must affect materialization, not committed tree identity. Merge must\nbe computed against full trees even when the local instance sees a subset, and\nout-of-view conflicts must be reported rather than silently omitted. Multiple\ninstances should share immutable objects but keep independent HEAD, index, view,\ndirty state and operation log.\n\n### Centralized authority with offline work\n\nLore's remote is authoritative for canonical branch tips, authorization and\ndurability, but normal stage/commit/branch/switch/diff operations remain local.\nA push uploads missing fragments first, then advances the remote branch tip with\none compare-and-swap. Interrupted uploads are resumable; unattached fragments are\nharmless; a raced pointer update refuses.\n\npm-vcs Phase 3 should implement the same safety shape without requiring one global\ncentral server:\n\n1. negotiate the missing reachable object or fragment set;\n2. upload it independently and resumably;\n3. verify closure and hashes remotely;\n4. atomically compare-and-swap the named remote ref; and\n5. leave local history untouched when publication loses a race.\n\nBundles remain the offline and air-gapped transport. A remote is an authority for\nits own refs, not the sole possible owner of history.\n\n### Multi-tenant isolation\n\nLore partitions storage access. Identical bytes may be physically deduplicated,\nbut knowing a hash does not grant access to another repository's copy. Sessions\nare authorized to partitions, and mutable state is partition-scoped too.\n\nThe hosted pm-vcs forge must copy this security invariant. Object existence,\ntiming and deduplication must not become cross-tenant oracles. Every read, write,\ncopy, list and ref operation must carry an authorized repository partition. Local\nrepositories do not need tenancy machinery; the served backend does.\n\n### Links, layers and repository composition\n\nLore has two distinct composition mechanisms:\n\n- A link is committed. It mounts a selected revision/subset of another repository\n  and carries that relationship to every clone.\n- A layer is local. It overlays content into a path for one instance and does not\n  change the committed revision.\n\npm-vcs should adapt both rather than revive Git submodules. A committed link must\npin an immutable repository/revision identity, declare path mapping, and preserve\nthe linked repository's authorization boundary. A local layer must never alter\ncommit IDs and must be visibly excluded from status unless explicitly promoted.\n\n### Locks\n\nLore exposes acquire, status/query and release operations for files that cannot be\nmerged. Its current roadmap says scalable enforcement is still in progress, so\npm-vcs must distinguish the desired contract from current Lore maturity.\n\npm-vcs locking belongs at the remote publication boundary. A local advisory lock\ncannot prevent another machine from pushing. Lock identity should use stable\n`FileId`, not path, so a rename cannot evade it. Leases need owner, branch scope,\nexpiry, renewal, explicit break authority and append-only audit records.\n\n### Lifecycle deletion and obliteration\n\nLore can remove payload bytes while preserving their addresses and returning a\ntyped obliterated state. Stable file context scopes deletion without rewriting the\nentire Merkle history. This supports leaked-secret cleanup and regulatory erasure.\n\npm-vcs currently never deletes objects. Phase 6 garbage collection handles\nunreachable storage, but it is not enough for mandatory erasure of reachable\nhistory. pm-vcs needs a separate obliteration design with typed absence, audit,\nauthorization, dedup isolation and explicit consequences for checkout and verify.\nIt must never pretend an obliterated object is ordinary corruption or not-found.\n\n### Repository serving and change review\n\nEpic Lore's glossary defines a change request as revisions proposed for review,\nbut the current public CLI reference does not expose a complete forge workflow.\npm-vcs Phase 5 should therefore learn from the concept, not claim compatibility.\nReview state remains a pm-vcs record; a portable patch series remains informed by\nkernel lore; the served repository adds authenticated discovery, review, checks\nand landing over the Phase 3 transport.\n\n## Capability decision matrix\n\n| capability | Epic Lore | pm-vcs now | decision | tracked destination |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| arbitrary byte files | first-class | shipped as blobs | retain | core |\n| text diff/merge above storage | built in, replaceable | shipped | retain and make adapters explicit | core |\n| structured record merge | not a storage primitive | shipped | retain as differentiator | core |\n| immutable CAS + mutable pointers | core split | shipped locally | retain | core / Phase 3 |\n| stable branch IDs | UUID separate from name | names only | adopt | Phase 3 |\n| stable file IDs | context beside hash | shipped with copy provenance and merge conflicts | retain and scale | core |\n| content-defined chunking | FastCDC | absent | adopt after benchmarks | Phase 6 |\n| fixed-size chunks/range reads | supported | absent | adopt | Phase 6 |\n| per-fragment compression metadata | Zstd today | whole-object deflate | adapt | Phase 6 |\n| sparse views/lazy fetch | default model | absent | adopt | Phase 6 after Phase 3 |\n| shared local immutable store | supported | per-working-tree store | adopt | Phase 6 |\n| central remote authority | intentional | absent | adapt to per-remote authority | Phase 3 |\n| resumable fragment transport | core | whole bundle only | adopt | Phase 3 + Phase 6 |\n| multi-tenant partitions | core security boundary | absent | adopt for served backend | Phase 5 |\n| file locking | present; enforcement scaling evolves | absent | adopt with stable IDs and leases | Phase 5/6 |\n| links | committed repository composition | absent | adapt | Phase 6 |\n| layers | local overlays | absent | adapt | Phase 6 |\n| obliteration | typed payload removal | absent | adopt separately from GC | Phase 6 |\n| change requests | named concept | planned native review records | adapt | Phase 5 |\n| operation log and undo | not the defining model | shipped | retain as differentiator | core |\n| stable change IDs across rewrite | not the defining model | shipped | retain as differentiator | core |\n| peer-to-peer-only design | explicit non-goal | bundles permit direct exchange | reject as a restriction | Phase 3 keeps both |\n| Lore format/protocol compatibility | public/versioned | independent format | reject | never a compatibility goal |\n\n## PM-linked file-change attribution\n\nThe pm CLI already lets an item link project files, tests and docs. The general\npm-vcs engine must make those links answerable against its own history without\nshelling out to Git.\n\n### Data contract\n\nEach committed file entry carries a stable `FileId`. Each commit already has a\nstable `ChangeId`. The PM attribution index is derived, not authoritative:\n\n```\nPM item id -> linked path at item event time\n           -> FileId resolved in the relevant tree\n           -> ChangeIds whose tree delta touches that FileId\n           -> commits/revisions carrying those changes\n```\n\nThe item file and append-only PM history remain ordinary versioned content. pm-vcs\ndoes not mutate a PM item merely because a file changed. Attribution is computed\nfrom immutable commits plus PM history, so two agents cannot race to update a\nsecondary database.\n\n### Required commands\n\n- `pm vcs items <from>..<to>`: native pm-vcs history to touched PM items.\n- `pm vcs files <item-id> [range]`: linked files resolved to stable IDs and the\n  changes that touched them.\n- `pm vcs changes <item-id>`: stable change identities associated with the item.\n- `pm vcs trace <path-or-file-id>`: moves, copies, changes and linked PM items.\n- `pm vcs commit --item <id>`: validates the item exists and records an explicit\n  item association in commit metadata without editing the item.\n\nPath-only links remain readable for old history. Once a path resolves to a\n`FileId`, later moves follow identity. Copies fork attribution: the copied file\ngets a new ID and a provenance edge to the source; it does not silently inherit\nevery future association.\n\n### Acceptance boundary\n\n1. An arbitrary text or binary file can be linked from a PM item and committed.\n2. A move preserves its file identity and the item link still resolves.\n3. A copy gets a new identity while retaining a source-provenance edge.\n4. Two agents change different linked files on different branches; merge retains\n   both changes and reports one item with both stable change IDs.\n5. Item edits and file edits on different branches merge without requiring a\n   derived attribution file to be reconciled.\n6. A deleted file remains traceable in historical ranges.\n7. Sparse instances can answer from indexes or report which missing fragments\n   are required; they never return a silently incomplete attribution.\n8. The native command's result is tested against independently walked trees and\n   PM history on real repositories.\n\n## Roadmap changes implied by the research\n\n### Phase 3: transport\n\nAdd stable remote/repository/branch identities; capability and format-version\nnegotiation; resumable missing-object transfer; remote closure verification; and\none final compare-and-swap publication. Keep bundles as a peer/offline transport.\n\n### Phase 4: self-hosting\n\nThe self-hosted bundle must include arbitrary source and binary fixtures, file\nmoves, PM-linked files and native TOON records. The gate compares file bytes,\nmodes, stable file IDs and PM attribution, not only paths.\n\n### Phase 5: served repository and forge\n\nAdd tenant partitions, authorization on every object/ref operation, non-leaking\nexistence checks, review/change-request records, checks, protected landing and\nremote lock leases. Kernel-lore patch series and Epic-Lore change requests stay\ndistinct concepts.\n\n### Phase 6: scale and lifecycle\n\nExpand beyond packs/reachability/GC: chunk lists, content-defined\nand fixed chunking, range reads, compression independence, shared stores, sparse\nviews, lazy fetch, links, layers and typed obliteration. GC and obliteration are\ndifferent operations with different safety contracts.\n\nThe implementation boundaries are tracked independently so one broad scale epic\ncannot hide incomplete behavior:\n\n- [`pm-vcs-ag8y`](../features/pm-vcs-ag8y.toon): stable file identity\n  and native PM-linked change attribution;\n- [`pm-vcs-vmmx`](../features/pm-vcs-vmmx.toon): streaming fragment\n  storage, chunking, range reads and resumable transfer;\n- [`pm-vcs-0lec`](../features/pm-vcs-0lec.toon): sparse and lazy\n  instances with a shared immutable store; and\n- [`pm-vcs-8xis`](../features/pm-vcs-8xis.toon): committed links,\n  local layers and typed obliteration.\n\n## Explicit non-goals\n\n- No Git, Jujutsu, Subversion or Lore object/wire compatibility.\n- No shell-out to another VCS from the pm-vcs engine.\n- No text-only assumptions below diff/merge adapters.\n- No mandatory always-online workflow.\n- No cross-tenant access merely because a content hash is known.\n- No derived PM attribution database that becomes a second source of truth.\n- No claim that Epic Lore's roadmap items are already production-complete.\n\n## Review triggers\n\nRevisit this document when Epic Lore reaches 1.0, changes its public format or\nprotocol contract, ships scalable lock enforcement/VFS/links, or exposes a full\nchange-request workflow. Until then, Lore is prior art and research input, not a\ndependency of pm-vcs."
